md`# brain-kernel

The Worker at the Brain's address. It serves the notebook, signs people in with atproto, works out who is calling, and forwards everything else to the core with that answer attached.

It holds no application logic. The caller it names is one of \`owner\`, a DID, or \`anonymous\` (a token is who made it, with its delegation's id), and nothing a caller sends can set it.

## People

A person is an atproto account, named by its DID. There are two roles. The **owner** is the DID this Brain was installed for. A **member** is a DID the owner added. Anyone else who signs in is nobody.

| a caller | may call |
|---|---|
| the owner, in their own tab | everything |
| the owner, through a turn token or a PDS | everything but \`infra.*\`, \`secret.get\`, \`token.*\`, \`grant.*\`, \`people.put\`, \`people.remove\`, \`people.link\`, \`people.unlink\`, \`rule.put\`, \`rule.delete\` |
| a member | \`inbox.reply\`, \`static.*\`, \`blob.*\`, \`portal.token\`, \`portal.modules\`, \`member.whoami\`, \`member.services\`, \`member.deploy\`, \`member.remove\`, \`member.modules\`, any method of a member's service (\`com.lopecode.brain.m.…\`, decided by its rule in the core), and the methods of their own grant (\`grant.put\`) |
| a DID that is not a member | the methods of its grant |
| anyone, through a portal token | the methods of one member's Workers, as themselves (see Portals) |
| anyone, on a method outside \`com.lopecode.brain\` | that method, as \`anonymous\`. A token sent with it is not read. |

| | |
|---|---|
| \`people.list\` | query, owner. \`{ people }\`: each \`{ did, handle, role, name, addresses, methods }\`. \`role\` is \`owner\`, \`member\` or \`null\` (linked an address, not added). |
| \`people.put\` | procedure, owner's session. \`{ did, name, services, quota, tags }\` makes that DID a member; a field left out keeps what it was. \`services: false\` stops their deploys. \`quota\` is \`{ workers, bytes, calls, deploys }\`; \`deploys\` is calls of \`member.deploy\` an hour. \`tags\` is \`[{ tag, access: "read" | "write" }]\`, the file tags they may read or change beside their own. |
| \`people.remove\` | procedure, owner's session. \`{ did }\` removes the member, their grant, their addresses and the delegations they made, and has the deployer delete their Workers. Their files stay, for the owner. |
| \`member.deploy\`, \`member.remove\`, \`member.modules\` | procedure, a member. Passed to the deployer with the caller's DID as the author; the owner is refused and deploys with \`infra.apply\`. \`member.deploy\` takes \`{ module, source, reason, was }\` and \`member.remove\` \`{ name, reason, was }\`: \`reason\` is 3 to 300 characters and is logged, \`was\` is the hash the caller saw running, \`null\` for no Worker. |
| \`people.link\` | procedure, owner's session. \`{ channel, address, did }\` binds an address without the sign-in below. |
| \`people.unlink\` | procedure, owner's session. \`{ channel, address }\` |
| Storage | \`rows\`: \`member/<did>\`, \`address/<channel>/<address>\`, \`grant/<did>\` (one from before 2026-10-10, until it is moved), \`handle/<did>\`, \`logins/<10 minutes>\`, \`logins-known/<10 minutes>\` |

**Addresses.** A channel is a Worker \`brain-x-NAME\` that appends what it receives to the inbox with \`body.address\`, the sender as that channel knows them (a phone number for \`whatsapp\`). The channel says nothing else about the sender. Which DID an address belongs to is decided here: \`address/NAME/<address>\`, where \`NAME\` comes from the Worker the core named as the entry's sender, not from anything in the entry. A channel can therefore speak only for addresses on its own channel.

**DIDs as addresses.** On \`bluesky\` the address is the sender's DID. \`people.put\` writes \`address/bluesky/<did>\` for the member it adds, and the owner's DID is the owner's without a row. Each change to People sends \`bluesky.know { all }\`, the DIDs of every member, to \`brain-x-bluesky\`. The rows are the kernel's own, which no Worker can read or write, and they are read under the name of the Worker the core identified by its key: an entry from \`brain-x-evil\` carrying the owner's DID is looked up as \`address/evil/<did>\` and names nobody.

**Linking.** A channel sends a stranger \`https://HOST/link?channel=NAME&code=CODE\`. The page asks them to sign in with atproto, shows the address the code stands for, and binds it to the signed-in DID when they press Link. The kernel learns the address by calling the channel's \`NAME.claim\` with the code; the answer is taken only from the Worker \`brain-x-NAME\`. Linking does not make anyone a member. When the signed-in DID is the owner, Link also asks for the last 4 characters of the address, because whoever writes from it is then answered as the owner. The page refuses to be shown in a frame.

**Sign-in links** (added 2026-10-09). For a program with no browser, such as Claude Code on the web. \`token.link\` (procedure, owner's session) takes \`{ name, methods, minutes, hours, deploy, unattended, daily }\` and answers \`{ name, deploy, unattended, link, until }\`, with \`daily\` when one was set; \`minutes\` (480 if left out; 10 until 2026-10-10, which a session that first had to have its network opened ran past) is how long the link is good, \`hours\` (8 if left out) how long its token lasts. A link made with \`deploy\` takes at most 480 minutes and 24 hours; one without, 10080 minutes and 168 hours (a week each, since 2026-10-10: a guest's link waits in a colleague's mail). \`daily\` is US dollars a UTC day that the token may spend of the owner's allowance, as in \`token.create\`; a token that asks for more and is replaced keeps it, and ends no sooner than the one it replaces; what it has spent that day is counted from nothing again, because the count is by delegation. The link is \`/auth/link?code=…\`. A GET of it answers text that says what to do and spends nothing, so a preview of a pasted link does not use it. The first POST answers \`{ name, token, methods, until, deploy, unattended }\` and deletes the link; a second answers 401. The token is one as \`token.create\` makes, with an end: it may not name \`infra.*\`, \`secret.*\`, \`token.*\`, \`grant.*\` or \`people.*\`, and \`token.list\` gives it with \`by: "link"\` until it ends. \`token.revoke\` with \`{ name }\` ends the token early, and deletes a link of that name nobody has used. A link is refused a name that a token or another link has.

**A token is a delegation** (2026-10-10). \`token.create\`, and the POST of a sign-in link, make a delegation with a secret holder in the core (\`delegation.create\`), and the kernel keeps no row for it. \`token.list\` and \`token.revoke\` read and end those and nothing else: a token made before is a row here until it is first used, or tokens are next listed or revoked, or a link is made, and is then handed to the core by the hash of its secret (\`delegation.adopt\`) and is a delegation like any other. A call with such a token is sent to the core with the delegation's id, and there it is the call of whoever made it (the owner, for one from \`token.create\`): their rules and their allowance, which \`daily\` in \`token.create\` (US dollars a UTC day) caps. It is not the owner's own tab, so what it makes in a store is kept under \`d:<id>\` and not under \`token:NAME\`; a link made with \`unattended\` is the owner's tab for the methods its list names by a name or a prefix, and not for one that only \`*\` reaches. A token ends within 5 s of \`token.revoke\`, the time a core instance remembers a row. The core's limits are now a token's: at most 50 methods, and a name that no other delegation of the owner has (400 from \`token.create\`, where two tokens of one name were made before). With \`delegate\` among its caps (made with \`delegation.create\` in the owner's or a member's session) a token reaches \`delegation.create\`, \`list\` and \`revoke\`, and the core says what it may make; \`token.list\` marks it \`delegate: true\`. \`token.list\` shows the owner's own tokens: a member's, and one made under another, are in \`delegation.list\`. While the core does not answer, such a token is refused with 503, on \`infra.*\` too: a sign-in link's token cannot deploy a core that is down, and the owner's session can.

**A grant is a delegation** (2026-10-10). \`grant.put\` makes a delegation that the DID holds, named \`grant\`, in the core, and the kernel keeps no row; \`grant.list\` and \`grant.delete\` read and end it, and \`people.remove\` ends it with the member. For each call a DID makes the kernel asks the core for that DID's grant. A grant from before is a row here until the DID next calls, grants or people are listed, or it is put again; one the core refuses (over 50 methods, or a name that is not letters and digits between dots) stays a row and gives nothing. \`people.sync\` tells the core who is a member and no longer what each was granted.

**Lists of methods** (2026-10-10). In \`token.create\` and \`token.link\` an entry of \`methods\` is a whole name or a prefix that ends \`.*\`: \`browser.*\` is every method of that service, \`*\` every method of this Brain. A name may go on after a \`/\` (\`ai.v1/chat/completions\`), and \`ai.v1/*\` is every method under \`ai.v1/\` and not \`ai.run\` (2026-10-10: the first shape check had refused every name with a \`/\`). No prefix reaches a name a token may not have (\`infra\`, \`secret\`, \`token\`, \`grant\`, \`people\`), whether it goes on with a \`.\` or a \`/\`. A grant to a DID still names whole methods.

**Other origins** (2026-10-10). No cookie is read, so any origin may call with a token and read the answer. A refusal is readable too: until 2026-10-10 an answer to a call with no \`authorization\` header carried no \`access-control-allow-origin\`, so a page with no key saw a network error where curl saw 401. A preflight is answered with the headers it asked for (\`access-control-request-headers\`), because a client library adds its own: the OpenAI one sends \`x-stainless-*\`. That holds when the names are letters, digits and \`-\`; a preflight that asks for any other (\`x_trace_id\`) is told \`authorization, content-type\` only, as every preflight was before. A page on another origin can read four headers of an answer: \`x-brain-price\`, \`x-ai-usd\`, \`x-ai-neurons\` and \`x-proxy-upstream\` (before 2026-10-10, the last only).

With \`deploy: true\`, \`token.link\` makes a link whose token also calls \`infra.*\` as the owner's session does, and these four only: \`infra.apply\`, \`infra.getState\`, \`infra.redistil\` and \`infra.confirm\` (which ends the probation of a kernel, core or page it deployed). Not \`infra.shell\`: that puts HTML at \`/\` with no reason, no \`was\` and no approval, and the page at \`/\` runs where the owner's session is kept; so a program cannot renew the page's shell, and the owner does after it deploys the page. It deploys and removes any Worker of this Brain, the kernel among them. What holds a session's apply holds its apply: a reason, \`was\`, and approval on the deployer's page (which needs the recovery key) when deploys need approval. The token itself names no secret, token, grant or people method. That is a limit only while deploys need approval: with approval off it can deploy a kernel of its own writing, and the kernel is what names the caller, so the token is then the owner in effect. Only a link makes such a token, so it always ends, in 24 hours at most; \`token.create\` cannot. Each call is logged by the kernel as \`infra.by.link\` with \`d:\` and the id of the token's delegation. Tom, 2026-10-09: "I do want singin links to be able to deploy with the authority of me".

With \`unattended: true\` beside \`deploy: true\` (added 2026-10-09), nothing that token applies, removes or redistils waits for approval, a Worker that declares Cloudflare permissions among them, so it changes which Cloudflare token a Worker is minted; the deployer logs \`by: token:NAME\`. It also reaches \`infra.shell\`. Such a token is the owner in effect for as long as it lasts: it can deploy a kernel of its own writing with nobody asked. What bounds it: it ends (8 hours unless said, 24 at most), \`token.revoke\` ends it, every change is logged with its reason, \`was\` holds, and the deployer mints only the groups in its \`GROUPS\`. Tom, 2026-10-09: "I would like LLMs to be able to deploy without approval and also choose what services they have".

Such a token's delegation has the cap \`session\`, and the core reads that from the row: for the methods its list names by a name or a prefix the call is the owner's own tab, so a rule of \`caller.session\` holds and a browser or a container it opens is the owner's. A method that only \`*\` reaches is not called from the owner's tab, so \`*\` does not reach \`rule.put\`, \`price.put\`, \`quota.put\` or \`logs.query\`; the list must name them (changed 2026-10-10 12:27, where before \`*\` reached them). The kernel does not rewrite the caller and has no \`call.by.link\` line: the core's line has the delegation's id. A deploy does not pass the core, so the kernel logs \`infra.by.link\` with \`d:<id>\`. A token reaches its methods and no path, so \`/llms.txt\` is read with no \`Authorization\` header. Ended links and tokens are deleted when the next link is made. Only the hash of the code and of the token is kept. The code is in a query string, which Cloudflare keeps beside each log line; it is good once.

**Asking for more** (added 2026-10-10). A program whose token names too little asks, and the owner answers in their own tab. \`token.request\` (procedure, anyone, and any token whatever it names) takes \`{ methods, caps, hours, reason, publicKey }\`, and \`name\` when no token is sent. \`caps\` are \`session\`, \`deploy\` and \`unattended\`, the last only with the other two; \`deploy\` and \`unattended\` as in \`token.link\` are read when \`caps\` is left out. \`publicKey\` is an RSA key of 2048 bits or more, SPKI DER in base64. It answers \`{ id, kind, name, url, code, until }\`: \`kind\` is \`upgrade\` when a token asked and \`new\` otherwise; \`url\` is \`/auth/request?id=…\`; \`code\` is the first 6 hex of the SHA-256 of the key, \`ABC-DEF\`, which the program shows its user so the owner can tell their request from someone else's. \`token.requested?id=\` (query, anyone, any token) answers the request and its \`status\`: \`pending\`, \`approved\` with \`granted\` and \`sealed\`, or \`denied\`. To the owner's session it also says what an upgrading token holds now. \`token.requests\` (owner's session) lists those waiting. \`token.approve\` (owner's session) takes \`{ id, hours, caps }\` or \`{ id, deny: true }\`: fewer hours and fewer caps than asked, never more. The answer is a sign-in link as \`token.link\` makes, good for 30 minutes, kept as its hash, carrying its caps, and \`sealed\`: RSA-OAEP SHA-256 to the key, in base64. The link is never kept in clear and only the key opens it. A delegation is not widened in place: for an upgrade the link's delegation has the token's scope and caps and what was granted, it is named \`NAME-XXXX\` from the token's name, and its POST revokes the token that asked once the new one is made, so the program is not left with neither. The program polls; nothing here pushes. \`/auth/request\` refuses frames, writes what a request says as text, signs the owner in and back (\`/auth/login\` takes \`next=/auth/request?id=…\`), and closes its tab after an answer when the browser lets it. A request ends 60 minutes after it is made; 20 new requests and 5 of each token wait at most. Rows \`request/<id>\`. Tom, 2026-10-10: "a lightweight way of you create a UI for adding scopes … and I can approve an upgrade (with my bluesky login hosted on the cb4 domain)".

**Turns.** \`inbox.list\` and \`inbox.poll\` answers pass through the kernel, which adds \`who: { did, role, handle }\` to each entry whose address is linked. For the owner's own session \`inbox.poll\` also adds \`turn\`, a token for each entry whose sender is the owner or a member. A call that carries it as \`Authorization: Bearer\` is made as that DID, with \`x-brain-via: turn:<entry id>\`. It lasts 10 minutes, ends with "sign out everywhere", and is refused once its DID is no longer a member.

**Rooms.** A channel's entry may carry \`body.room: { id, name, members }\`: the message was written where others read the answer, and \`members\` are their addresses on that channel. A turn for such an entry is capped by who is in the room, because its answer is read by all of them.

| in the room | the turn may call |
|---|---|
| the sender alone | what the sender may |
| anyone else | \`inbox.reply\`, and a method only when the sender and every other participant has it: the owner has every method, a member the methods of their grant, anyone else none |

So the owner being in a room raises nothing, one participant who is not a member leaves \`inbox.reply\` alone, and the methods every member has by default (\`static.*\`, \`blob.*\`, \`member.*\`, a member's own services) are refused in a room: they answer with the caller's own files and services, which the others in the room cannot read. No path that is not a method is served to a room's turn.

When \`inbox.poll\` hands out the turn, the kernel asks the channel for the room as it is then (\`NAME.room { id }\`, answered only by \`brain-x-NAME\`) and resolves each address as it resolves a sender. The DIDs go into the turn token; member rows and grants are read at each call, so a grant withdrawn or a member removed during a turn is refused from then on. A channel that does not answer, a list that leaves out the sender, one marked incomplete or longer than 50: each counts as a participant who is not a member. A room the channel says it no longer serves gets no turn. The entry gains \`room: { id, name, served, people }\` for the page.

A channel can narrow a turn with this and cannot widen one: the cap is taken inside what the sender may call, and the sender is still found under the channel's own name.

**Members in the core.** After \`people.put\`, \`people.remove\`, \`grant.put\` and \`grant.delete\` the kernel sends the core every member with their grant, limits and tags (\`people.sync\`), because the core checks a member's Worker as its author.

**Sessions.** No cookie is set or read. After atproto sign-in \`/auth/callback\` answers a page that writes a session token to this origin's \`localStorage\` (\`brain_session\`) and goes on; the Brain's page sends it as \`Authorization: Bearer\` with every call. \`/auth/login\` resolves a handle and calls a PDS for anyone, so it is counted: 60 starts in 10 minutes for all visitors together, then 429. Starts for the handle of the owner or of a member, once they have signed in here, are counted apart, 600 in 10 minutes, so a flood of other handles does not keep them out. 600 starts that name the owner's own handle do keep the owner out, until the 10 minutes end. It is a signed \`{ did, exp, epoch }\`, lasts 30 days and is not renewed: after that the visitor signs in again. "Sign out" drops it from the page; "sign out everywhere" (\`/auth/logout?everywhere=true\`) raises the epoch, which ends every session, turn and portal token issued before. Because a caller is only ever who their token says, a page on another address has nothing of this Brain's to send, and \`Origin\` is not looked at. A link or an \`<img>\` cannot carry a token: a private file is fetched by the page and shown from the bytes. The secret that signs all three kinds of token is still named \`COOKIE_KEY\`.

Before 2026-10-07 13:40 the session was a \`SameSite=Lax\` cookie. \`NAME.SUBDOMAIN.workers.dev\` addresses are one site, so a page on another Worker's address sent it: on cb4 the owner's cookie with \`Origin: https://evil.endpointservices.workers.dev\` got 200 from \`GET library.list\` and \`POST token.revoke\`.

**Portals.** \`portal.token { author }\`, called with a session by the owner or a member, answers \`{ token, author, id, expires }\`: a 10-minute token naming the caller as the viewer and one member as the author. A call carrying it is made as the viewer with \`x-brain-via: portal:<id>\`, and reaches only the methods of that member's Workers (\`com.lopecode.brain.m.<id>.…\`, where the Worker's own rule then decides) and \`portal.modules\`, which answers that member's module sources and \`cloudflare-iac\`. When the viewer is the author it also reaches \`member.whoami\`, \`member.services\`, \`member.deploy\`, \`member.remove\` and \`member.modules\`. It is refused once the viewer or the author is no longer a member. The page gives it to a sandboxed frame (\`brain-shell\`, "Portals"), so a member's code never holds the viewer's session.

Limits: an entry is stamped when it is read, so an address linked after its message arrived is known from then on. The kernel does not record which turn made a call.`
// %%
kernel_service = cloudflare.Service("kernel", kernelApp, { role: "kernel" })
// %%
// Announced to the page, so the Services list shows it. A cell of its own: a deployed function cannot depend on it.
kernel_announce = {
  plugins.add("workers", kernel_service, { invalidation });
  return "announced kernel_service";
}
// %%
// @atcute's OAuth client, identity resolvers and signature check as one ES module (77 KB). Travels as lib/atcute.js.
atcute = library("atcute", FileAttachment("atcute.js"))
// %%
// A page's session: a signed note of who signed in, which the page keeps and sends as Authorization: Bearer.
// The atproto tokens stay in the kernel's rows.
sessionToken = {
  const enc = new TextEncoder();
  const b64u = (bytes) => btoa(String.fromCharCode(...bytes)).replace(/\+/g, "-").replace(/\//g, "_").replace(/=+$/, "");
  const sign = async (key, text) => {
    const k = await crypto.subtle.importKey("raw", enc.encode(key), { name: "HMAC", hash: "SHA-256" }, false, ["sign"]);
    return b64u(new Uint8Array(await crypto.subtle.sign("HMAC", k, enc.encode(text))));
  };
  return {
    make: async (key, did, { ttl = 30 * 24 * 3600 * 1000, epoch = 0 } = {}) => {
      const payload = b64u(enc.encode(JSON.stringify({ did, exp: Date.now() + ttl, epoch })));
      return `v1.${payload}.${await sign(key, payload)}`;
    },
    read: async (key, token, epoch = 0) => {
      const [v, payload, sig] = String(token || "").split(".");
      if (v !== "v1" || !key || !payload || sig !== (await sign(key, payload))) return null;
      try {
        const claims = JSON.parse(atob(payload.replace(/-/g, "+").replace(/_/g, "/")));
        return claims.exp > Date.now() && (claims.epoch || 0) === epoch ? claims.did : null;
      } catch {
        return null;
      }
    }
  };
}
// %%
// One inbox entry's turn: who it is for and which entry, signed with the session key under its own label.
turnToken = {
  const enc = new TextEncoder();
  const b64u = (bytes) => btoa(String.fromCharCode(...bytes)).replace(/\+/g, "-").replace(/\//g, "_").replace(/=+$/, "");
  const sign = async (key, text) => {
    const k = await crypto.subtle.importKey("raw", enc.encode(key), { name: "HMAC", hash: "SHA-256" }, false, ["sign"]);
    return b64u(new Uint8Array(await crypto.subtle.sign("HMAC", k, enc.encode("turn:" + text))));
  };
  return {
    // room: the DIDs of everyone else who reads the answer, null for one who is nobody here.
    make: async (key, did, entry, { ttl = 600000, epoch = 0, room } = {}) => {
      const payload = b64u(enc.encode(JSON.stringify({ did, entry, exp: Date.now() + ttl, epoch, ...(room ? { room } : {}) })));
      return `t1.${payload}.${await sign(key, payload)}`;
    },
    read: async (key, token, epoch = 0) => {
      const [v, payload, sig] = String(token || "").split(".");
      if (v !== "t1" || !key || !payload || sig !== (await sign(key, payload))) return null;
      try {
        const claims = JSON.parse(atob(payload.replace(/-/g, "+").replace(/_/g, "/")));
        return claims.exp > Date.now() && (claims.epoch || 0) === epoch ? { did: claims.did, entry: claims.entry, ...(Array.isArray(claims.room) ? { room: claims.room } : {}) } : null;
      } catch {
        return null;
      }
    }
  };
}
// %%
// A portal's token: who is looking (did) and whose services they are looking at (author, and that author's ID).
// Signed with the session key under its own label, so it is neither a session nor a turn.
portalToken = {
  const enc = new TextEncoder();
  const b64u = (bytes) => btoa(String.fromCharCode(...bytes)).replace(/\+/g, "-").replace(/\//g, "_").replace(/=+$/, "");
  const sign = async (key, text) => {
    const k = await crypto.subtle.importKey("raw", enc.encode(key), { name: "HMAC", hash: "SHA-256" }, false, ["sign"]);
    return b64u(new Uint8Array(await crypto.subtle.sign("HMAC", k, enc.encode("portal:" + text))));
  };
  return {
    make: async (key, did, author, id, { ttl = 600000, epoch = 0 } = {}) => {
      const payload = b64u(enc.encode(JSON.stringify({ did, author, id, exp: Date.now() + ttl, epoch })));
      return `p1.${payload}.${await sign(key, payload)}`;
    },
    read: async (key, token, epoch = 0) => {
      const [v, payload, sig] = String(token || "").split(".");
      if (v !== "p1" || !key || !payload || sig !== (await sign(key, payload))) return null;
      try {
        const claims = JSON.parse(atob(payload.replace(/-/g, "+").replace(/_/g, "/")));
        return claims.exp > Date.now() && (claims.epoch || 0) === epoch ? { did: claims.did, author: claims.author, id: claims.id } : null;
      } catch {
        return null;
      }
    }
  };
}
// %%
// A call forwarded by a PDS carries a JWT signed with the caller's atproto key. Checked against their DID document.
verifyServiceJwt = async (bearer, nsid, audience, known) => {
  const b64u = (t) => Uint8Array.from(atob(t.replace(/-/g, "+").replace(/_/g, "/").padEnd(Math.ceil(t.length / 4) * 4, "=")), (c) => c.charCodeAt(0));
  const [h, p, s] = bearer.split(".");
  let claims;
  try {
    claims = JSON.parse(new TextDecoder().decode(b64u(p)));
  } catch {
    return { error: "BadJwt", message: "not a JWT" };
  }
  if (claims.aud !== audience && claims.aud !== audience + "#brain") return { error: "BadJwtAudience", message: "aud " + claims.aud };
  if (!(claims.exp > Date.now() / 1000)) return { error: "JwtExpired", message: "exp " + claims.exp };
  if (claims.lxm !== nsid) return { error: "BadJwtLexiconMethod", message: "lxm " + claims.lxm };
  if (typeof claims.iss !== "string" || !claims.iss.startsWith("did:")) return { error: "BadJwtIssuer", message: "iss" };
  // The DID document is fetched only for an account that could call something: a stranger's JWT makes no request.
  if (known && !(await known(claims.iss.split("#")[0]))) return { error: "AuthRequired", message: "this account has no access to this Brain" };
  const resolver = new atcute.CompositeDidDocumentResolver({ methods: { plc: new atcute.PlcDidDocumentResolver(), web: new atcute.WebDidDocumentResolver() } });
  const doc = await resolver.resolve(claims.iss.split("#")[0]).catch(() => null);
  if (!doc) return { error: "BadJwtIssuer", message: "no DID document" };
  const vm = (doc.verificationMethod || []).find((m) => m.id.endsWith("#atproto"));
  if (!vm) return { error: "BadJwtIssuer", message: "no #atproto key" };
  const ok = await atcute.verifySig(atcute.parsePublicMultikey(vm.publicKeyMultibase), b64u(s), new TextEncoder().encode(h + "." + p), { allowMalleableSig: true });
  return ok ? { iss: claims.iss.split("#")[0] } : { error: "BadJwtSignature", message: "signature does not verify" };
}
// %%
kernelApp = {
  const app = new hono.Hono();
  const NS = "/xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.";
  // A body of a known small size is read before it is sent on: a stream the core does not read to its end throws
  // "Can't read from request stream after response has been sent" here once the answer is out (cb4, 2026-10-08).
  const bodyOf = async (c) => (+(c.req.header("content-length") || Infinity) <= 1e6 ? c.req.raw.arrayBuffer() : c.req.raw.body);
  const fail = (c, status, error, message) => c.json({ error, message }, status);
  app.onError((e, c) => fail(c, 500, "InternalServerError", String((e && e.message) || e)));
  // What a portal token reaches beside the methods of its author's Workers, when the viewer is that author.
  const PORTAL_OWN = ["member.whoami", "member.services", "member.deploy", "member.remove", "member.modules"].map((m) => "com.lopecode.brain." + m);
  // A method of some member's own service. Its rule, which the deployer wrote, says whether another member may call it.
  const MEMBER_NS = "com.lopecode.brain.m.";
  // Any token may ask for more, and read the answer (Asking for more).
  const ASKS = ["com.lopecode.brain.token.request", "com.lopecode.brain.token.requested"];

  // Every caller sends a token in a header, this Brain's own page included, so any origin may call and read the answer.
  const cors = { "access-control-allow-origin": "*", "access-control-allow-headers": "authorization, content-type", "access-control-allow-methods": "GET, POST", "access-control-expose-headers": "x-brain-price, x-ai-usd, x-ai-neurons, x-proxy-upstream", "access-control-max-age": "600" };
  app.use("/xrpc/*", async (c, next) => {
    // A client library adds headers of its own (the OpenAI one: x-stainless-*). The preflight is told the ones it
    // asked for: no cookie is read and no header but authorization says who calls, so none of them is a risk.
    const asked = c.req.header("access-control-request-headers") || "";
    if (c.req.method === "OPTIONS") return new Response(null, { status: 204, headers: { ...cors, ...(/^[A-Za-z0-9, -]{1,1000}$/.test(asked) ? { "access-control-allow-headers": asked, vary: "access-control-request-headers" } : {}) } });
    await next();
    // Every answer, a refusal too: without these a page reads a 401 as a network error.
    const headers = new Headers(c.res.headers);
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(cors)) headers.set(k, v);
    c.res = new Response(c.res.body, { status: c.res.status, headers, webSocket: c.res.webSocket });
  });

  // Nothing a browser sends by itself says who is calling: no cookie is read. A caller is whoever their token says.
  // The key that signs session, turn and portal tokens is SESSION_KEY. It was COOKIE_KEY until 2026-10-07, and a
  // Brain installed before then has that name bound. A token is signed with the first name bound and read with either.
  const signingKeys = async () => {
    const bound = async (read) => {
      try {
        return (await read()) || null;
      } catch {
        return null;
      }
    };
    return [...new Set([await bound(() => secrets.SESSION_KEY), await bound(() => secrets.COOKIE_KEY)].filter((k) => typeof k === "string" && k))];
  };
  const signingKey = async () => (await signingKeys())[0];
  const readToken = async (kind, bearer, epoch) => {
    for (const key of await signingKeys()) {
      const read = await kind.read(key, bearer, epoch);
      if (read) return read;
    }
    return null;
  };
  const identify = async (request, nsid) => {
    const auth = request.headers.get("authorization") || "";
    if (!auth.startsWith("Bearer ")) return { caller: "anonymous", via: "" };
    const bearer = auth.slice(7), named = (did) => (did === config.owner ? "owner" : did);
    if (/^[vtp]1\./.test(bearer)) {
      const epoch = (await rows.get("epoch")) || 0;
      if (bearer.startsWith("v1.")) {
        const did = await readToken(sessionToken, bearer, epoch);
        return did ? { caller: named(did), via: "session", did } : { refused: { error: "AuthRequired", message: "this session has expired or is not this Brain's" } };
      }
      if (bearer.startsWith("t1.")) {
        const turn = await readToken(turnToken, bearer, epoch);
        return turn ? { caller: named(turn.did), via: "turn:" + turn.entry, did: turn.did, turn: true, room: turn.room || null } : { refused: { error: "AuthRequired", message: "this turn token has expired or is not this Brain's" } };
      }
      const portal = await readToken(portalToken, bearer, epoch);
      return portal ? { caller: named(portal.did), via: "portal:" + portal.id, did: portal.did, portal } : { refused: { error: "AuthRequired", message: "this portal token has expired or is not this Brain's" } };
    }
    if (bearer.split(".").length === 3) {
      const v = await verifyServiceJwt(bearer, nsid, "did:web:" + config.host, async (did) => did === config.owner || !!(await rows.get("member/" + did)) || !!(await grantOf(did)));
      return v.error ? { refused: v } : { caller: v.iss === config.owner ? "owner" : v.iss, via: "jwt", did: v.iss };
    }
    // A token is a delegation that the core keeps, found by the hash. The caller is who made it. Its scope is the
    // list here, three of its caps are deploy, unattended and delegate, and the core is sent its id. One made before
    // 2026-10-10 is a row here until it is first met, and is then handed to the core.
    const hash = await sha256(bearer), old = await rows.get("token/" + hash);
    if (old && old.until < Date.now()) return { refused: { error: "AuthRequired", message: "this token has expired" } };
    const d = old ? await move("token/" + hash, old) : await resolve(hash);
